WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.893
Narrator: It's a song you've
all heard at least once.

00:00:02.893 --> 00:00:05.600
♪ Is this the real life? ♪

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.762
And it was probably not like
anything you've heard before.

00:00:08.762 --> 00:00:11.450
♪ Is this just fantasy? ♪

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:14.260
I'm of course talking about
Queen's legendary single

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:18.290
"Bohemian Rhapsody," a song
that, even 40 years later,

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.060
is one of the most influential
and memorable songs

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.561
of our generation.

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:25.800
But have you ever wondered
why this six-minute single

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.110
that no one ever thought would be a hit

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.848
became one of the most
famous songs ever written?

00:00:30.848 --> 00:00:33.765
♪ I'm just a poor boy ♪

00:00:33.765 --> 00:00:35.959
♪ I need no sympathy ♪

00:00:35.959 --> 00:00:37.979
♪ Because I'm easy come ♪

00:00:37.979 --> 00:00:38.812
(record scratching)

00:00:38.812 --> 00:00:41.870
"Bohemian Rhapsody" was a
song long in the making,

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.020
but it officially kicked into
gear in the summer of 1975,

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.830
when Freddie Mercury began writing it

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:51.280
as an operatic piece titled, "Real Life."

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.440
After the success of their last
album "Sheer Heart Attack,"

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.710
Queen was given complete creative freedom

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.910
and control over their next piece.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.580
And it's obvious that they
took that creative freedom

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:02.690
and ran with it.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:07.560
Fisch: "Bohemian Rhapsody" had
a very rare effect on people,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.120
which is that it was
one of those songs where

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.170
the first time you heard it,

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:13.890
you hadn't heard anything like it.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.350
Narrator: This is Irwin Fisch.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.130
He's an Emmy-nominated composer

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.510
and professor at NYU Steinhardt.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:21.890
Fisch: In my image is
that it's the kind of song

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:24.770
that makes you pull over
to the side of the road,

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:28.290
because you go, "What the devil is this?"

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:32.210
Very few songs have
done that, and that did.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:33.760
Narrator: And he's right.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.430
"Bohemian Rhapsody" was
different for its time

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.680
and still is today.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.930
Unlike most pop hits that
lasted around three minutes,

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:45.847
it was a six-minute pop
single that has an opera,

00:01:45.847 --> 00:01:48.600
an opera, right in the middle of the song.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.140
Fisch: It actually in some
ways hasn't been influential,

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.900
because it was so fully realized

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.800
that it was a little bit of,
"Where do we go from here?"

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.270
It managed to become a
ubiquitous part of the culture

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.650
and something that
never gets off the radio

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.260
and never stops in the karaoke
bars and is used in movies,

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:10.470
and it's all over the place,
because nobody has still done

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.290
anything that sounds like that.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:14.470
Narrator: One of the reasons
why "Bohemian Rhapsody"

00:02:14.487 --> 00:02:17.271
sounds so different is in its structure.

00:02:17.271 --> 00:02:19.761
The song is neither an a cappella,

00:02:19.761 --> 00:02:21.111
a ballad,

00:02:21.111 --> 00:02:22.102
an opera

00:02:22.103 --> 00:02:23.485
or rock.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:27.610
It's actually all of them in one song.

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:31.800
Fisch: It advanced a tradition
of suites in pop music,

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.960
meaning not a continuous song,

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.060
not a verse, chorus, verse,
chorus, bridge structure,

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.260
which was the norm.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.270
A put together group of
different songs, in essence.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.220
So if people refer to
"Bohemian Rhapsody" as a song,

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.660
that's a bit of a misnomer.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.420
It's actually three or four songs.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.620
Narrator: "Bohemian Rhapsody"
can actually be divided

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:55.380
into five different sections:
an a cappella introduction.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.720
♪ Is this the real life ♪

00:02:58.720 --> 00:02:59.553
Ballad.

00:02:59.553 --> 00:03:02.025
♪ Mama ♪

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:03.283
Opera.

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:04.760
♪ Thunderbolts and lightning ♪

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.820
♪ Very very frightening me ♪

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:07.960
Hard rock.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:11.850
♪ So you think you can stone
me and spit in my eye ♪

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.458
And finally a reflective coda.

00:03:14.458 --> 00:03:17.790
♪ Nothing really matters ♪

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:20.500
It was also highly unusual
for a popular single

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.030
to not include a chorus, while combining

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:25.660
different musical styles and lyrics.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:29.860
It is by definition a
mind-blowing genre bender.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:34.130
Fisch: This innovation
started around the mid-'60s.

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:37.300
It started basically with the
Beach Boys and the Beatles.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.379
Beach Boys with "Good Vibrations."

00:03:39.379 --> 00:03:42.480
♪ I'm picking up good vibrations ♪

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.330
♪ She's giving me the excitations ♪

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.208
The Beatles with "A Day in the Life."

00:03:47.208 --> 00:03:51.625
♪ A crowd of people stood and stared ♪

00:03:53.683 --> 00:03:57.300
Epic songs that pieced
together different ideas

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.000
into a cohesive whole.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.400
Queen, in "Bohemian
Rhapsody," took that idea

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.990
and pushed it way over the top.

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:09.230
Narrator: And to see just
how over the top they went,

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:10.870
you need to look no farther than

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.380
this operatic section of the song.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:17.360
The lyrics name characters from
classical Italian theaters,

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.770
quotes from the Quran,
and the demon Beelzebub.

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:23.310
And this section that
sounds like it's been sung

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:27.720
by a roomful of choir was
actually just three people:

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.930
Freddie Mercury, drummer Roger Taylor,

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:32.290
and guitarist Brian May.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:36.630
It's not just the vocals;
there's harmony everywhere,

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:39.933
even in the instruments that
almost sound like echoes.

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:44.640
This technique was heavily
inspired by a production method

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.110
called the "Wall of
Sound," developed in 1960

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:50.060
by producer Phil Spector.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.580
He put masses of musicians in one room,

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:55.020
three keyboard players
playing the same part

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:56.910
but in various similar instruments,

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:59.910
like the harpsichord or an electric piano,

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.870
and recording them
together to create a sound

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:05.990
the likes of which had
never been heard before.

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:09.180
That was exactly what
Queen wanted to accomplish.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.490
Fisch: When people talk
about what a great song

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:14.410
"Bohemian Rhapsody" is,
they're talking equally,

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:16.690
or even more, about the production.

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:18.200
Narrator: To achieve the
sound that they wanted,

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.350
Queen used a technique
known as reduction mixing,

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:23.293
also called ping pong recording.

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:25.940
Most of the pop songs you listen to today

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:29.380
use a lot of audio tracks,
each track reserved

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:31.690
for different instruments and vocals,

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:34.170
combining to make one song.

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:36.150
But back then, technology
limited the amount

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.350
of audio tracks that could be used.

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:40.770
For example, Beatles'
legendary "Sgt. Pepper's"

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:43.620
was recorded on an
analog four-track record.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.910
And to fit more than four
tracks in a four-track record,

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:50.040
they would record all
four tracks, then bounce

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:55.040
all their tracks into one,
record, bounce again, and repeat.

00:05:56.570 --> 00:06:00.400
The bounce tracks would combine
all of the tracks into one,

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:03.500
meaning if you raise the sound
of that particular track,

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.210
it would raise the volume of all

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.050
the individual tracks within.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:08.810
Fisch: Part of the great
challenge of that process

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:13.700
was that you had to make
commitments to your mix,

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:17.010
to the blending of
everything as you went along,

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:19.240
so you needed to have a lot of foresight

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.490
and a great image of where you were going.

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:24.650
By the time Queen made
"Bohemian Rhapsody,"

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:27.090
we were up to 24-track tape.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.460
By today's standards, that's
still not many tracks.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:31.910
They had so many vocals and they had

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:33.850
so many layers of guitars.

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:38.050
I've heard that they had
about 180 individual tracks

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:41.620
that got put onto a
24-track, two-inch tape.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:43.030
Narrator: But of course this method

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:46.090
of bouncing tracks came
with its own challenges.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:47.840
Once it's done, you can't go back

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.830
to just fix it, like we can do now.

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:53.410
Fisch: Two-inch, 24-track tape
that they were working on,

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:55.160
it was a physical process.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:57.080
It was a razor blade.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:00.950
It was an edit block where
the tape would sit there.

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:03.600
You would slice through the two-inch tape.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.350
You would cut out what
you wanted to cut out.

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:06.610
And you would splice it together

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:08.580
with a little piece of white tape.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:11.540
Now it's very easy digitally.

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:13.140
You chop it on the screen.

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.300
If you made a mistake, you can fix it.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.410
Everything now is non-destructive.

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.680
Everything they did then was destructive,

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.920
so it took a lot of commitment
and a lot of knowledge

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:26.750
and a very, very intense, deep skillset

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:29.020
to be able to piece that stuff together

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:30.470
and have it sound smooth.

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:32.880
Narrator: Just how much
tracking went into the song

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.770
becomes more evident when
you remove the instruments

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:37.813
to just listen to the vocals.

00:07:38.671 --> 00:07:42.085
♪ I'm just a poor boy ♪

00:07:42.085 --> 00:07:44.238
♪ I need no sympathy ♪

00:07:44.238 --> 00:07:48.366
♪ Because I'm easy come, easy go ♪

00:07:48.366 --> 00:07:51.930
♪ Little high, little low ♪

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.350
Fisch: And before the Beatles
and before the Beach Boys,

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:56.370
a song was a song.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:59.570
It needed to be presentable on the piano.

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:01.950
If you sat down and
played "Bohemian Rhapsody"

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:05.197
from start to finish on the
piano, you probably would say,

00:08:05.197 --> 00:08:09.540
"Wow, that's really wild and interesting."

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:12.920
But you probably wouldn't
say that's going to be a hit

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:15.440
that's gonna endure for 40 years.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:18.850
What made it that had a lot to do

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:20.300
with the sound they created.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.400
Narrator: And of course,
it's hard to talk about

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:24.470
"Bohemian Rhapsody" without talking about

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:27.780
the man behind the song, Freddie Mercury,

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:31.650
because this song was
his baby, his brainchild.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:34.379
Unlike most of Queen's
songs that were written

00:08:34.379 --> 00:08:37.140
collaboratively in the
studio, this was a song that,

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:39.270
according to the guitarist Brian May,

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:43.590
was "all in Freddie's head"
before it even began recording.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:46.160
Fisch: Freddie Mercury
talked about the song

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:50.620
in an interview as
"experimentation in sound."

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:54.190
I think that could be taken
to mean that the experiment

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.820
was to see if he could
get what was in his head,

00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:00.710
his sonic preconceptions, out there.

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:02.550
I don't think it was the
kind of experimentation

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:04.830
where they went into
the studio to just see

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:08.630
what would happen, because
he was famously buttoned-up

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:11.480
and had the production and the notes

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:15.010
and the arrangements and sound
of the thing in his head.

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:18.050
I think the experiment
was really about seeing

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:22.120
if something unique could
be realized in the studio.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:25.950
An important reason that
"Bohemian Rhapsody" resonates

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:29.940
and has resonated for over 40 years

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:33.800
is that it embodied
something very intense,

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:37.550
which is Freddie Mercury's
personality and life.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:42.510
That record is an oral
extension of Freddie Mercury's

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:45.060
self-consciousness without shame.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.660
It's music in some ways the sensibilities

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:49.240
are out of the closet.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:51.650
As a performer, there hadn't been

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:53.800
a Freddie Mercury before Freddie Mercury.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:56.320
Narrator: And perhaps
beyond all the notes,

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:59.380
lyrics and performances, what truly makes

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:02.690
"Bohemian Rhapsody"
great is that it embodies

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:05.200
what every musical piece should be:

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:08.560
the talent and the
drive to push boundaries

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.250
and create something
that brings us together,

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:13.520
even 40 years later.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:15.950
In a time where pop
songs just all generally

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:18.120
sound the same, maybe that's why we still

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.300
can't stop listening
to "Bohemian Rhapsody."

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:24.530
With just this one song,
Freddie Mercury and Queen

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:26.950
became something that very few artists

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:30.073
managed to achieve: a legend.

